{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788179512,"nonce":"Nzo/Nb0TMiW7iVLuqJDFGuSkKj4=","title":"1.8 advance drives the game clock: unpause to play, pause to stop","message":"Wave 1 — substrate. Depends: 1.3 (the advance it replaces), 1.7 (dialog halt).\nSupersedes the tick mechanism only; the advance CONTRACT and lifecycle stay.\n\nGoal\n\n`advance` should set the game's own time speed and let RimWorld tick, then\npause — instead of pinning `CurTimeSpeed = Paused` every frame and pumping\n`DoSingleTick()` by hand inside `GameComponentUpdate`.\n\nWhy, recorded honestly\n\nEvan called this out 2026-08-31 and he is right. The manual loop was chosen in\nspike 0.1 to beat frame starvation (a parked window ticking at 1-2 fps = ~4\ntps). That problem was fixed SEPARATELY by `render_unfocused` in the launcher,\nand nobody re-examined the loop afterwards. The three defences offered for it\ndo not survive contact:\n\n- \"Immune to TimeSlower, so raids do not slow it.\" Hollow: a raid triples\n  per-tick cost, so the budget loop crawls through a raid anyway. The\n  multiplier was never the binding constraint — CPU per tick is.\n- \"The thermal governor needs a continuous rate.\" Thermals are a hardware-level\n  concern on one machine, and the governor is already INERT on BORGES (no\n  sensor). It should never have shaped the API.\n- \"Exact tick counts.\" Nobody needs them. Report actual ticks; overshoot of a\n  few ticks is immaterial to every consumer, rwtest included.\n\nAnd one thing gets strictly BETTER by unpausing, which is the real argument:\ntoday's loop DEFEATS vanilla's own force-pause. TimeDriver's comment says it\noutright — \"we pin CurTimeSpeed=Paused and drive DoSingleTick ourselves, so\nnothing pauses us\" — which is the entire reason 1.7 needed a per-TICK modal\ncheck. Unpaused, a force-pausing window really does pause the game, and\n`LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters` stops being starved by construction rather\nthan by our vigilance.\n\nScope\n\n- Set `CurTimeSpeed` at advance start; restore `Paused` on every exit path\n  (ticks, until-match, timeout, dialog, error, abandon).\n- Stop checks move from per-tick to per-FRAME in the existing\n  GameComponentUpdate slot. Overshoot is bounded by `TickRateMultiplier * 2`;\n  REPORT actual ticks run, never a promised exact figure.\n- `speed: normal|fast|superfast|ultrafast`. Keep accepting `max_tps` so 1.4's\n  CLI and the existing acceptance scripts do not break: map it to the nearest\n  speed and report BOTH what was asked and what was actually set.\n- The thermal governor no longer scales a frame budget. Minimal faithful\n  translation: it steps the speed down one notch and says so in the result. It\n  must not reintroduce a continuous rate.\n- Remove the per-frame `CurTimeSpeed = Paused` re-pinning and the `repinned`\n  accounting; simplify 1.7's per-tick modal check to whatever is still needed\n  once vanilla pauses itself.\n- `slower_spans` reporting stays — TimeSlower now genuinely applies, so report\n  it as an observed fact rather than as something we overrode.\n\nThe hazard that is genuinely new\n\n`TickManager.CurTimeSpeed`'s setter and `Pause()` route through\n`PlayerCanControl`, and during cutscenes and landing confirmations the setter\nSILENTLY NO-OPS (it shows a message instead). FINDINGS section 10 already flags\nthis. So every set is set-then-VERIFY `Find.TickManager.CurTimeSpeed`, never\nassume; a refused set is reported, not swallowed. Today's per-frame re-pinning\naccidentally papered over this and the new code has no such cover.\n\nAcceptance\n\n- `advance {ticks:20000}` runs with the game genuinely UNPAUSED (observable in\n  status.json as a non-Paused speed while it runs), returns with the game\n  paused, and reports actual ticks run plus the overshoot. Overshoot is at most\n  `TickRateMultiplier * 2`.\n- Each of the four speeds is exercised and its measured tps reported, replacing\n  1.3's tps table. State plainly if a speed does not deliver what vanilla's\n  multiplier predicts.\n- `until:letter` still halts on the letter, and the halted journal seq still\n  names the event; the halt tick is within the bounded overshoot of the event\n  tick.\n- THE 1.7 REGRESSION, explicitly: drive a timing-out letter during an advance.\n  The game force-pauses itself, `advance` returns `reason:\"dialog\"` naming the\n  window, and a SECOND timing-out letter afterwards still arrives — proving\n  `OpenAutomaticLetters` was never starved. This is the test 1.7 exists for and\n  it must pass without a per-tick guard doing the work.\n- A `CurTimeSpeed` set that is refused by `PlayerCanControl` is reported rather\n  than silently producing a stalled advance.\n- `pause` still interrupts an advance in flight; busy-gating unchanged.\n- Zero red errors across the whole run.","files":null}]}